class Solution {
public:
    int numFactoredBinaryTrees(vector<int>& arr) {
        const int MOD = 1000000007;
        int n = arr.size();
        sort(arr.begin(), arr.end());
        unordered_map<long long, int> dp;
        for (int i =  0; i < n; ++i) {
            dp[arr[i]] = 1;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            for (int j = 0; j < i; ++j) {
                long long a = arr[j ];
                long long b = arr[i];
                if (b % a == 0) {
                    long long c = b / a;
                    if (dp.count(c)) {
                        dp[b] = (dp[b] + 1LL * dp[a] * dp[c]) % MOD;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        int ans = 0;
        for (auto& p : dp) {
            ans = (ans + p.second) % MOD;
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
```   The code sorts the array and uses an unordered_map to store the number of ways to form trees for each value. It processes numbers in increasing order, ensuring that when computing dp for a number, all possible child values have already been computed. For each pair of previous numbers, it checks if their product equals the current number and adds the product of their ways if so. Finally, it sums all dp values modulo MOD. This handles the examples correctly and respects the constraints. The unordered_map is used for O(1) lookups, and the nested loop is O(n^2) which is acceptable for n<=1000.  Potential issues like overflow are avoided by using long long for calculations and taking modulo at each step.  The approach correctly counts ordered pairs of children, matching the example 2 where [10,2,5] and [10,5,2] are distinct.  It also correctly handles cases where a number can be factored in multiple ways, like 8 with factors 2 and 4.  The code is ready to submit as it conforms to the required class structure.

