class Solution {
public:
    int ladderLength(string beginWord, string endWord, vector<string>& wordList) {
        unordered_set<string> words(wordList.begin(), wordList.end());
        if (words.find(endWord) == words.end()) return 0;

        queue<pair<string, int >> q;
        q.push({beginWord, 1});
        unordered_set<string> visited;
        visited.insert(beginWord);

        while (!q.empty()) {
            auto [word, level] = q.front();
            q.pop();
            if (word == endWord) return level;

            for ( int i = 0; i < word.size(); ++i) {
                char original = word[i];
                for (char c = 'a'; c <= 'z'; ++c) {
                    word[i] = c;
                    if (words.find(word) != words.end() && visited.find(word) == visited.end()) {
                        visited.insert(word);
                        q.push({word, level + 1});
                    }
                }
                word[i] = original;
            }
        }
        return 0;
    }
};
```   This solution uses BFS to explore word transformations level by level, ensuring we find the shortest path. We use a queue to hold words along with their transformation level, and a visited set to prevent revisiting words. The dictionary is converted to an unordered_set for O(1) lookups. For each word, we generate 26 possible one-letter changes per position and check if the new word is in the dictionary and unvisited. If we reach the endWord, we return its level immediately. If the queue empties without finding endWord, we return 0. This approach efficiently handles the constraints with time complexity around O(N * L * 26), where N is the number of words and L is the length.

